DA-004 — Scene 10 — Raw Audio — Approved Draft v1.1

By the time the windows began to gray, Eli had copied the camera files to his laptop and lined the batteries along the desk to charge.

Martin sat on the edge of the nearer bed with his shoes on.

Neither of them had slept.

The Rider Room looked ordinary again in the weak morning light. One chair remained where Martin had admitted moving it. The other sat at the angle Eli had found when he came back from charging the equipment.

He looked at them once while the first video loaded. The chairs were done. Whatever he had thought when he saw them out of place belonged to the gap in the recording and to the few seconds before Martin answered his question. Martin had moved them. Nothing they had heard afterward changed that.

Eli put on his headphones.

“You want these?”

Martin shook his head. “Play it out loud.”

Eli unplugged them.

The laptop speakers filled the room with the smaller version of the night.

Ventilation.

Fabric against clothing.

The low handling noise of Eli changing his grip on the camera.

His own voice, too close to the microphone.

Martin’s voice farther away.

They listened through part of the room session first.

The overhead rumble that had seemed to move through the ceiling during the investigation came through as a broad low vibration.

Martin leaned toward the computer.

“That one.”

Eli stopped the file.

“The rumble?”

“Yeah.”

He backed it up and played it again.

Through the laptop speakers it sounded less directional than Eli remembered. A low rolling sound, followed by a softer vibration and the scrape of someone moving in the room.

Martin pointed toward the screen.

“That could be the same thing we heard near the elevator.”

“Could be.”

“Or plumbing.”

“Could be.”

Eli marked the time in his notes.

He did not write explained.

Martin noticed.

“You’re not calling that one?”

“No.”

“Why?”

“Because we didn’t find the source.”

Martin considered that.

Eli added, “But I’m not putting it in a clip like it came out of nowhere either.”

Martin nodded.

They moved forward.

A door clicked somewhere in the recorded corridor.

A guest voice crossed the microphone too faintly to make out.

The elevator mechanism produced a brief vibration that both of them recognized immediately.

Martin said, “That’s useful.”

Eli looked at him.

“What?”

“Having the ordinary ones.”

Eli almost laughed.

“You spent half the night complaining that I recorded too much.”

“I complained that you talked too much.”

“That’s different?”

“Yes.”

Eli let the file continue.

For a few minutes they were father and son watching badly framed hotel footage before breakfast, correcting each other about which sound belonged to which hallway.

Then the first knock sequence came.

One impact.

A pause.

Two more.

Eli stopped moving.

Martin did too.

On the laptop, the sounds were dull. That was the first thing Eli noticed. Not quiet or hidden. Dull. Three short impacts with none of the shape the room had seemed to give them at the time.

He played the section again.

The first knock came through clearly enough.

Then the pause.

Then two.

Martin looked toward the wall where they had first heard them hours earlier.

Eli watched him do it.

“Sounds different,” Martin said.

“From what?”

“Being here.”

Eli replayed it.

The laptop gave them no useful distance. No convincing wall. No bathroom side. No sense that the final two had come from somewhere different than the first.

Just impacts captured from one recording position.

Eli opened the waveform and enlarged the section.

Martin leaned over.

“You can tell anything from that?”

“I can tell where the sound is in the file.”

“That’s not what I asked.”

“No.”

Eli zoomed back out.

He listened once more without watching the screen.

One.

Pause.

Two.

He could still hear the old rhythm.

He could not tell whether he would have heard it that way if he had not already known what to listen for.

Martin sat back.

“Can you measure the gap?”

“Yeah.”

Eli reached for the trackpad, then stopped.

“What?”

Martin waited.

Eli looked at the three visible peaks.

“I can measure what the microphone got.”

“That’s what I meant.”

“I know.”

He checked the approximate spacing, then wrote it down without announcing it as a verdict.

The peaks gave him intervals in the recording. They could not tell him how either man had grouped the sounds before recognizing the pattern.

The recording could tell him when the impacts reached the microphone. It could not tell him what surface made them, how far away that surface had been, or whether the apparent spacing had carried the same shape before two tired people recognized it.

Martin read the number over his shoulder.

“That prove anything?”

“No.”

“Rule anything out?”

“No.”

Martin nodded.

Eli continued through the night.

The post-confession recurrence was not there.

They both knew why.

The camera had been charging.

The recorder had not been running.

There was only their memory of sitting in the room after Martin said the rhythm had come from nowhere but him, and then hearing it. Eli had nothing to play back.

He marked the gap anyway.

Martin watched him.

“You writing that we heard it?”

“Yes.”

“Without a recording.”

“Yes.”

“Good.”

Eli glanced at him.

Martin looked toward the laptop rather than back.

The separation test came next.

Long corridor footage.

Ventilation.

An elevator.

A television behind a guest-room door.

Eli’s breathing when the nausea started.

His voice describing where he stood.

The faint sound of the lollipop wrapper against his hand.

Nothing in the recording made the candy movement more useful than it had been while he was holding it.

When they reached the voice-like section, Eli raised the volume slightly.

Martin leaned closer.

At first there was only the corridor.

Then something faint entered under the ventilation.

Eli stopped the file.

“Here.”

He backed up.

They listened again.

A wavering sound.

Human-sounding.

Maybe.

It rose briefly, separated into something like two overlapping tones, and faded.

Martin frowned.

“That’s what you followed?”

“That’s part of it.”

“Can you get words?”

“I couldn’t then.”

Eli played it again.

The tour guide’s stories were still available in his memory. Children in the upper corridors. Voices. Candy.

The words were available too.

If he wanted them.

He did not say them.

Martin leaned toward the speaker.

“Again.”

Eli replayed it.

Martin shook his head.

“I don’t have anything.”

“Talking?”

“Maybe.”

“Crying?”

Martin waited longer before answering.

“Could be.”

Eli lowered the volume.

He did not open an audio filter.

He did not type a candidate phrase into the notes.

The sound had reached him after hours of hearing the hotel described in advance, after repeated listening, after the lollipop, after nausea, after the private cadence had already changed what both men were waiting for.

Whatever the microphone contained, Eli no longer had a first listen to give it.

He wrote:

Human-sounding audio. No stable words.

Martin read it.

“That all?”

“That’s all.”

They moved to the final corridor file.

Eli knew the section before it arrived.

He knew the exact camera movement, the staff door entering frame, Martin’s hand against the painted surface.

He knew what came after.

On the recording, Martin knocked once.

The microphone caught it hard and close.

Pause.

Two knocks.

Those were hard and close too.

Martin watched himself lower his hand.

Neither of them spoke in the video.

The corridor noise continued.

Five seconds.

Ten.

Fifteen.

Twenty.

Eli could hear the slight movement of Martin’s shoes.

Then Martin’s recorded voice:

“Well.”

A moment later, the first new impact sounded.

Eli’s hand stopped on the trackpad.

Martin leaned forward.

The laptop speaker gave them a dull knock.

Nothing about it sounded as far behind them as Eli remembered.

Nothing about it sounded clearly left.

Nothing placed it near the guest hall.

The microphone had captured an impact.

That was all.

The pause followed.

Two impacts.

Martin stared at the screen.

Eli did not stop the playback.

Their recorded voices came next.

“That was behind us.”

“I thought farther left.”

The recording preserved their disagreement more clearly than either man’s sense of direction.

Martin pointed at the laptop.

“There.”

“I hear it.”

“It’s the same cadence.”

Eli let the footage continue through their corridor check, the open linen alcove, the empty visible hall, Martin trying to move farther, Eli stopping him.

When the video reached Martin’s “Okay,” Eli paused it.

For several seconds they listened to the fan in the room above the quiet laptop.

Martin said, “Back it up.”

“To the knocks?”

“Yes.”

Eli did.

They listened again.

Martin’s deliberate sequence.

The wait.

The later three impacts.

Again.

Martin leaned closer on the third pass.

Eli watched his face rather than the screen.

“What?”

“I’m trying to hear whether the second set is farther from the camera.”

“You can’t get distance like that from this.”

“I know.”

Martin sat back.

Eli closed the waveform.

“You can hear the knocks.”

“Yes.”

“You can hear the timing.”

“Yes.”

“You can hear us disagree about where they came from.”

Martin looked at him.

“Yes.”

Eli waited.

Martin rubbed his thumb against his forefinger.

He did not say answer.

He did not say ghost.

He looked instead at the row of file names in the folder.

“Send me those.”

Eli thought he meant the clips.

“Which ones?”

“All of it.”

“The whole night?”

“The raw files.”

Eli looked at him.

Martin shrugged once.

“I want to hear it on my own headphones.”

“You know I can make you a shorter—”

“No.”

Martin tapped one finger on the desk.

“Raw.”

Eli turned back to the laptop.

At the beginning of the night, that request would have felt like winning.

Martin wanted the recordings.

Martin wanted to listen again.

Martin had followed the knocks out of the room, knocked the rhythm back, and now wanted the original files in his own possession.

Eli could have made all of that mean something larger.

He opened the transfer window instead.

The progress bar began to move.

Martin stood and picked up his bag.

“You sending the camera originals too?”

“Yes.”

“Not the export?”

“The originals.”

“Good.”

Eli watched the first file finish.

Outside the window, the mountain had begun to separate from the low clouds. Cars moved through the wet parking lot below. Somewhere in the hallway a housekeeping cart struck a doorframe, followed by a woman saying something Eli could not make out.

Neither man turned toward the sound.

Martin zipped his bag.

“What are you going to say?”

Eli looked at him.

“About what?”

“The video.”

Eli glanced at the laptop.

The transfer continued.

“I don’t know yet.”

Martin nodded.

No challenge.

No correction.

Eli looked once more at the chairs.

One had been moved by Martin.

Some of the sounds from the night resembled ordinary noises they had found elsewhere in the building.

The distant voice-like recording still did not contain words he could defend.

The final knocks were in the file.

So was their disagreement about where those knocks had come from.

The file preserved the impacts.

It did not preserve an explanation.

The transfer finished.

Eli turned the laptop toward Martin.

“Sent.”

Martin checked his phone.

A moment later he nodded.

“Got it.”

Eli closed the computer.

They left the Rider Room with their bags and the camera between them.

Neither of them named what had made the rhythm.

